s self.init(os.getenv(self.name)); on exception re-raises same type with prefix f\"{self.name}: {e}\" (TypeError falls back to bare raise); then setattr(owner, self.name, value) caches, so later env changes do not affect value until rearm. Factory staticmethods: int(default=0)->_int, float->_float, bool->_bool, str->_str, str_or_none()->lambda v: v (returns None when unset), int_or_none(), choice(default, choices). New config DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS can follow str_or_none/int_or_none style but must validate >=32 and add a factory (e.g. EnvVar factory method) parsing int and raising ValueError mentioning the name; __get__ prepends the name anyway. `rearm(name)` does setattr(envs, name, _descriptors[name]) so next access re-resolves; `rearm_all()` loops all descriptors doing same. This preserves caching semantics: env edits alone do not change attribute, only rearm+reaccess does."}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Evidence ev-8554ade850ecb397d235 | deepinfer/environ.py | lines:31-94 | sha256=fe2c531989c419f0b51b169a07f5ed826dcec7f95c0c5bc3a351805923c4f218\nclass EnvVar[T]:\n    \"\"\"Non-data descriptor, resolved on first access\"\"\"\n\n    __slots__ = (\"name\", \"init\", \"lock\")\n\n    def __init__(self, init: Callable[[str | None], T]) -> None:\n        self.init = init\n        self.lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def __set_name__(self, owner: type[envs], name: str) -> None:\n        self.name = name\n        _descriptors[name] = self\n\n    def __get__(self, instance: envs | None, owner: type[envs]) -> T:\n        with self.lock:\n            cached = owner.__dict__.get(self.name, self)\n            if cached is not self:\n                return cached\n            try:\n                value = self.init(os.getenv(self.name))\n            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001\n                try:\n                    raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") from e\n            